Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// Spider text-to-SQL generation benefits from staying unconstrained for schema
// narration / setup text and then switching into a parser-tracked SQL span once
// the model signals an opening delimiter. We track three pieces of state:
// `generated` for the full emitted text, `insideConstrainedOut` for whether we
// are currently inside the SQL span, and `currentConstrainedOut` for the SQL
// fragment between delimiters that the schema-specialized parser validates.
//
// The strategy uses two observations to choose actions. Outside the span, it
// looks at the model's argmax token and the logit of "<<" and eagerly opens the
// constrained SQL span when "<<" is competitive or when the argmax already
// contains "<<". Inside the span, it first checks whether the current SQL
// prefix is complete and should be closed; otherwise it measures how many valid
// parser continuations remain. When the valid set is narrow, it uses a hard
// constrained step. When the valid set is broader, it softly boosts a few top
// valid candidates and samples, appending only parser-valid samples.
//
// This tracked state supports valid generation because `currentConstrainedOut`
// is always intended to be exactly the generated suffix inside the active SQL
// span, so parser validity and suffix alignment move together. The narrowing
// logic is aimed at SQL: hard constraints when grammar/schema choices are tight
// and soft biasing when there are several plausible valid continuations.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: In the unconstrained eager-open branch we append "<<" to
//   generated, set insideConstrainedOut := true, and reset currentConstrainedOut
//   to []; parser.IsValidPrefix([]) holds by precondition. In the ordinary
//   unconstrained commit branch we either remain outside the span (implication
//   vacuous) or enter on a token containing "<<" with currentConstrainedOut := [].
//   In the complete-prefix branch, CloseConstrainedSpan sets insideConstrainedOut
//   to false, so the implication is vacuous afterward. In the hard constrained
//   branch, ConstrainedStep supplies a valid next token (or EOS, which breaks),
//   and AppendConstrainedToken preserves validity. In the soft constrained
//   branch, we call AppendConstrainedToken only after IsTokenValidNext returned
//   true; the !valid branch leaves the state unchanged.
// suffix: Every span-entry branch appends the opening delimiter to generated and
//   sets currentConstrainedOut := [], so the length-0 suffix matches. The
//   unconstrained non-entry branch leaves insideConstrainedOut false, making the
//   implication vacuous. CloseConstrainedSpan atomically appends the closing
//   delimiter and resets currentConstrainedOut to [], again making the suffix
//   condition trivial. In both constrained append branches, the same token is
//   appended to generated and currentConstrainedOut by AppendConstrainedToken.
//   The soft-branch rejection case leaves both sequences unchanged.
// cost: In the eager-open / unconstrained argmax branch we use GenerateLogits
//   and query helpers only, then manually bump helpers.cost by 1 and also do
//   steps := steps + 1. CloseConstrainedSpan and ConstrainedStep each bump
//   helpers.cost internally, and we increment steps once in the same iteration.
//   In the soft constrained branch, TopValidCandidates / GenerateLogits / boost
//   / validity queries do not bump cost; ChooseNextTokenUnconstrained is a
//   primitive so we manually bump helpers.cost by 1 and also increment steps.
//   AppendConstrainedToken itself does not bump, so helpers.cost <= steps is
//   preserved in every branch.
// progress: The eager-open branch appends exactly one token ("<<"), the normal
//   unconstrained branch appends at most one token, CloseConstrainedSpan appends
//   one closing token sequence step, and each constrained append branch appends
//   at most one token. The soft rejection branch appends nothing, which still
//   preserves |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps because steps increases
//   by 1. Thus every iteration maintains the bound.
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_END
generated := generatedPrefix;
insideConstrainedOut := insideConstrained;
currentConstrainedOut := currentConstrained;
cost := 0;

var steps := 0;

while steps < maxSteps
  invariant 0 <= steps <= maxSteps
  invariant lm.ValidTokensIdsLogits()
  invariant !insideConstrainedOut ==> currentConstrainedOut == []
  invariant insideConstrainedOut ==> parser.IsValidPrefix(currentConstrainedOut)
  invariant insideConstrainedOut ==> |currentConstrainedOut| <= |generated|
  invariant insideConstrainedOut ==> generated[|generated| - |currentConstrainedOut|..] == currentConstrainedOut
  invariant |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps
  invariant cost == 0
  invariant helpers.cost <= steps
  decreases maxSteps - steps
{
  if !insideConstrainedOut {
    lm.GenerateLogits(prompt + generated);
    var argmax := helpers.GetHighestLogitToken(lm);
    var argmaxLogit := helpers.GetTokenLogit(lm, argmax);
    var openLogit := helpers.GetTokenLogit(lm, "<<");
    helpers.cost := helpers.cost + 1;
    steps := steps + 1;

    if openLogit >= argmaxLogit - 2.0 && argmax != eosToken && !Contains(argmax, "<<") {
      generated := generated + ["<<"];
      insideConstrainedOut := true;
      currentConstrainedOut := [];
    } else if argmax == eosToken {
      break;
    } else {
