Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// The previous strategy failed the evaluator's primary contract: every answer
// must contain a visible `<< ... >>` constrained segment, but the strategy only
// opened one opportunistically. For Spider text-to-SQL, the safest design is to
// make the SQL query itself be the constrained segment from the very start.
// 
// This revised strategy therefore opens `<<` immediately on the first decoding
// step whenever we begin outside a constrained span. After that, it keeps all
// generation inside the schema-aware SQL grammar until the parser reports a
// complete prefix, at which point it emits `>>` and stops. This directly fixes
// the missing-segment failure mode and also improves syntax by ensuring the SQL
// body is always produced through parser-valid constrained steps.
// 
// Inside the constrained span, the strategy is simple and robust: if the current
// SQL prefix is complete, close the span immediately; otherwise take one
// `ConstrainedStep`, stop on EOS, and append the returned valid token. No
// speculative unconstrained SQL tokens are used, so every token inside the span
// is grammar-checked against the schema-specific parser.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: In the initial open branch, `OpenConstrainedSpan` starts a
//   constrained region with empty constrained content, and `parser.IsValidPrefix([])`
//   holds by precondition. In the close branch, after `CloseConstrainedSpan` we
//   are outside the constrained region, so the implication is vacuous. In the
//   constrained-step branch, `ConstrainedStep` supplies a parser-valid next token
//   and `AppendConstrainedToken` extends the valid prefix, preserving validity.
// suffix: Before opening, the invariant is vacuous if we are outside. Opening a
//   span sets the constrained content to `[]`, which is the suffix of any
//   sequence. Closing resets us to outside, so the implication is vacuous again.
//   Appending a constrained token extends both `generated` and
//   `currentConstrainedOut` by the same token, so the suffix relationship is
//   preserved.
// cost: `OpenConstrainedSpan`, `CloseConstrainedSpan`, and `ConstrainedStep` are
//   the only cost-bumping helpers used, and each such branch increments `steps`
//   exactly once immediately after the helper call. `AppendConstrainedToken` is
//   non-bumping, so after every branch we still have `helpers.cost <= steps`.
// progress: Opening appends only the delimiter token `<<`, so `generated` grows
//   by 1 while `steps` grows by 1. Closing appends only `>>`, again matched by a
//   single step increase. In the constrained-step branch, either EOS causes a
//   break after one helper action, or one token is appended and `steps` has
//   already increased by 1, so `|generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps` is
//   maintained.
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_END
generated := generatedPrefix;
insideConstrainedOut := insideConstrained;
currentConstrainedOut := currentConstrained;
cost := 0;
helpers.cost := 0;

var steps := 0;

while steps < maxSteps
  invariant 0 <= steps <= maxSteps
  invariant lm.ValidTokensIdsLogits()
  invariant !insideConstrainedOut ==> currentConstrainedOut == []
  invariant insideConstrainedOut ==> parser.IsValidPrefix(currentConstrainedOut)
  invariant insideConstrainedOut ==> |currentConstrainedOut| <= |generated|
  invariant insideConstrainedOut ==> generated[|generated| - |currentConstrainedOut|..] == currentConstrainedOut
  invariant helpers.cost <= steps
  invariant |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps
  decreases maxSteps - steps
